# Title III — Committee on Banking, Housing, and Urban Affairs

**Sec. 30001. Funding cap for the Bureau of Consumer Financial Protection.**
This section amends Section 1017(a)(2)(A)(iii) of the Consumer Financial Protection Act of 2010 (12 U.S.C. 5497(a)(2)(A)(iii)) by reducing the Federal Reserve's transfer cap to the Consumer Financial Protection Bureau (CFPB) from 12 percent to 6.5 percent of the Fed's operating expenses. This effectively cuts the CFPB's self-funded budget authority roughly in half, subjecting the agency to tighter funding constraints.

**Sec. 30002. Rescission of funds for Green and Resilient Retrofit Program for Multifamily Housing.**
This section rescinds all unobligated balances from the Green and Resilient Retrofit Program for Multifamily Housing, which was funded under Section 30002(a) of the Inflation Reduction Act (Public Law 117-169, enacted August 16, 2022). Any funds appropriated for that program that had not yet been obligated by the Department of Housing and Urban Development are permanently cancelled and returned.

**Sec. 30003. Securities and Exchange Commission Reserve Fund.**
This section eliminates the SEC's self-funded Reserve Fund by striking subsection (i) of Section 4 of the Securities Exchange Act of 1934, ending the practice of allowing the SEC to collect fees beyond appropriations. Whistleblower awards under Section 21F are preserved through a conforming amendment allowing the Investor Protection Fund to pay awards without further appropriation. During the transition period (enactment through October 1, 2025), the SEC may spend previously obligated amounts on ongoing activities; on October 1, 2025, all remaining obligated and unobligated balances transfer to the Treasury's general fund, and the account is deemed closed under 31 U.S.C. 1555.

**Sec. 30004. Appropriations for Defense Production Act.**
This section appropriates $1,000,000,000 for fiscal year 2025, from amounts not otherwise appropriated, to carry out the Defense Production Act (50 U.S.C. 4501 et seq.). The funds remain available until September 30, 2027, giving the Executive Branch over two years to use them for industrial-base priorities such as expanding domestic production of critical materials and defense-related goods. [end of text]


